From: HP-UX 11.23 ia64 port notes
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] gcov-tool: HP-UX passes struct FTW to the nftw callback BY VALUE

HP-UX 11.23 DOES have nftw() — it is declared in <ftw.h> and present in libc
as a weak symbol. What differs is the callback's fourth parameter: HP declares

    int nftw(const char *, int (*)(const char *, const struct stat *,
                                   int, struct FTW), int, int);

i.e. `struct FTW` BY VALUE (see /usr/include/ftw.h), where POSIX and glibc pass
`struct FTW *`. gcov-tool's unlink_gcda_file therefore has the wrong prototype
and the build fails on the incompatible function-pointer argument.

Only gcov-tool uses nftw in gcc, and it ignores the parameter entirely, so
matching HP's declaration under __hpux is sufficient and changes no behaviour.

NOTE: this is deliberately NOT routed through libgnushim. gcc sits at the root
of the toolchain — libgnushim is built BY gcc — so a link-time dependency from
gcc onto the shim would invert the stack and tie the gcc depot to the shim
depot. A six-line __hpux prototype guard is also plausibly upstreamable; a
private compat library is not. Userland ports that hit the same divergence
should use libgnushim's rpl_nftw instead.
--- a/gcc/gcov-tool.c
+++ b/gcc/gcov-tool.c
@@ -60,7 +60,11 @@
 unlink_gcda_file (const char *name,
                   const struct stat *status ATTRIBUTE_UNUSED,
                   int type ATTRIBUTE_UNUSED,
+#ifdef __hpux
+                  struct FTW ftwbuf ATTRIBUTE_UNUSED)  /* HP-UX 11.23 nftw passes FTW by value */
+#else
                   struct FTW *ftwbuf ATTRIBUTE_UNUSED)
+#endif
 {
   int ret = 0;
   int len = strlen (name);
